WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.222
Every decision begins with a frame.

00:00:02.222 --> 00:00:06.466
The frame defines what question is being answered, what is inside scope,

00:00:06.466 --> 00:00:10.356
what is outside scope, who is affected, who owns the decision,

00:00:10.356 --> 00:00:12.124
and what time horizon matters.

00:00:12.124 --> 00:00:16.368
A weak frame can make even careful analysis point in the wrong

00:00:16.368 --> 00:00:20.612
direction because the team is solving a question that is too vague,

00:00:20.612 --> 00:00:23.441
too narrow, or simply not the real choice.

00:00:23.441 --> 00:00:25.917
Framing starts by distinguishing symptoms from decisions.

00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:30.161
A symptom might be slow incident response, rising cloud cost, missed deadlines,

00:00:30.161 --> 00:00:31.222
or learner disengagement.

00:00:31.222 --> 00:00:35.112
The decision is the choice the organization must make in response.

00:00:35.112 --> 00:00:39.002
Are we changing staffing, redesigning a process, buying tooling, reducing scope,

00:00:39.002 --> 00:00:41.124
changing priorities, or running an experiment?

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:45.368
Until the actual choice is named, discussion often drifts between complaints, opinions,

00:00:45.368 --> 00:00:46.429
and partial fixes.

00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:48.551
A good frame also identifies constraints.

00:00:48.551 --> 00:00:51.734
Constraints include budget, time, people, regulatory obligations, safety requirements,

00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:53.502
technical dependencies, and political realities.

00:00:53.502 --> 00:00:55.270
Naming constraints is not pessimism.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:59.514
It prevents the team from comparing options that cannot actually be executed.

00:00:59.514 --> 00:01:03.758
It also helps separate hard limits from assumptions that might be negotiable.

00:01:03.758 --> 00:01:05.526
Stakeholders and decision ownership matter.

00:01:05.526 --> 00:01:08.355
A decision that affects operations, security, customers, learners,

00:01:08.355 --> 00:01:12.599
or frontline teams should not be framed only from the viewpoint of

00:01:12.599 --> 00:01:14.721
the person with the loudest voice.

00:01:14.721 --> 00:01:17.904
At the same time, endless consultation can blur accountability.

00:01:17.904 --> 00:01:21.794
A clear frame says who gives input, who recommends, who approves,

00:01:21.794 --> 00:01:23.916
and who is responsible for acting.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:27.099
Finally, ask what happens if no decision is made.

00:01:27.099 --> 00:01:30.282
Inaction is still a choice, and it has consequences.

00:01:30.282 --> 00:01:32.050
Sometimes waiting creates useful information.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.526
Sometimes delay increases risk or closes options.

00:01:34.526 --> 00:01:38.062
The frame determines what evidence, options, and tradeoffs are considered,

00:01:38.062 --> 00:01:42.660
so improving the frame is one of the highest-leverage moves in decision practice.
